      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks! that worked, the error went away. but i just realized i also need the lambda to be able to check if the bucket exists before it tries to write, so i think i need to add something for list or head object? how would i add that to this same policy without making it too broad?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, you're looking to add a \"s3:ListBucket\" permission to your policy, which is a good idea to check if the bucket exists before attempting to write logs. However, you don't want to make your policy too broad, so you'll want to restrict the permission to only the necessary actions. Here's how you can modify your policy to include the \"s3:ListBucket\" action:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\",\n        \"s3:GetBucketLocation\",\n        \"s3:ListBucket\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-company-logs-bucket\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": \"s3:PutObject\",\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-company-logs-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nWith this updated policy, your Lambda function will be able to list the bucket and check if it exists, as well as write logs to it. Remember to keep your permissions as specific as possible to follow the principle of",
